>> title: Croissant Dough
>> category: Pastry & Doughs
>> tags: pastry, laminated, yeast, butter, make-ahead
>> counters: Bakery
>> aka: pâte à croissant, pate a croissant, croissant dough, viennoiserie dough, yeasted laminated dough, yeasted puff pastry
>> pairs-with: croissant, pain-au-chocolat
>> servings: 12
>> time: 14 hr
>> slack: unforgiving — butter colder than the dough shatters into shards and warmer smears into it, and either way the layers are gone for good
>> step.1: mix to a smooth dough, chill overnight
>> step.2: beat the block to a square, chill 20 min
>> step.3: fold the dough round the block and roll it long
>> step.4: fold in three, turn, chill 45 min — three times
>> step.5: rest 1 hour before it is cut

Mix @bread flour{3 3/4%cup}(500 g), @cold whole milk{1 1/4%cup}(300 mL), @granulated sugar{1/4%cup}(55 g), @instant yeast{2 1/4%tsp}(7 g), @table salt{2%tsp}(11 g) and @softened unsalted butter{3%Tbs}(40 g) to a smooth dough, knead it four minutes only, flatten it into a rectangle and ~chill{10%hr} overnight. The long cold ferment is where the flavour comes from, and it also brings the dough to the same firmness as the butter it has to carry.

Beat @cold unsalted butter{1 1/8%cup}(250 g) between two sheets of paper into a 7-inch square, then ~firm{20%min} in the #refrigerator{}. Half the flour's weight in butter is what makes this a croissant and not a bun; the block wants to bend like clay, not crack.

Roll @&(~2)chilled dough{} to a 10-by-14-inch rectangle, lay @&(~1)butter block{} on one half, fold the bare half over it and pinch the edges shut, then roll the parcel out to a long rectangle three times its width.

Fold @&(~1)parcel{} in three like a letter, turn it a quarter, roll it long again and ~chill{45%min} — three times in all. Three folds, not four: a fourth thins the layers past the point where the yeast can push them apart, and the pastry comes out of the oven closer to a palmier.

Rest @&(~1)laminated dough{}, wrapped, in the #refrigerator{} ~rest{1%hr} before it is cut. Cut warm, the layers smear shut at the knife and the crescent bakes solid.
